    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "T cells are crucial for the immune system to mount an adequate defense against foreign invasion. To be activated against a pathogen, antigen-presenting cells come into intimate contact with naive T cells and form the immune synapse (IS), a structure that facilitates MHC-TCR binding and exchange of signaling molecules. Actin-capping proteins modulate changes to the cytoskeleton, a process that allows for movement toward APCs and formation of the IS, although their role in T cells is largely understudied. We report here a study focused on understanding the dynamics of one of these capping proteins, CapZ, in regulating T cell movement and activation, both crucial processes in T cell defense against pathogen. Using gene-cloning techniques, we constructed a fluorescent CapZ fusion protein as well as an shRNA knockdown, and successfully expressed them in T cells using retroviral transduction. Using confocal microscopy, we show that CapZ localizes to the leading edge of motile T cells, whereas silencing of CapZ resulted in cells that moved faster, farther, and straighter. Examining formation of the IS with APCs, we found that CapZ localizes to the interface of the IS, and loss of CapZ in T cells results in cells that can form larger synapses with APCs. Further studies of the mechanism of CapZ in T cell activation may provide new, interesting approaches for manipulation of T cells in fighting infections and cancerous cells.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
